WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.411
SQL injection occurs when application input is used to construct a database query unsafely,

00:00:05.411 --> 00:00:08.066
allowing the input to alter query meaning.

00:00:08.066 --> 00:00:09.204
For JavaScript developers,

00:00:09.204 --> 00:00:14.894
the highest-value prevention pattern is to avoid building SQL by combining strings with untrusted values.

00:00:14.894 --> 00:00:18.309
The preferred control is parameterized queries or prepared statements,

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:21.723
where values are passed separately from the query structure.

00:00:21.723 --> 00:00:24.378
Query builders and ORMs can reduce risk,

00:00:24.378 --> 00:00:27.792
but they should not be treated as magic shields.

00:00:27.792 --> 00:00:33.862
Many libraries are safe for common patterns and unsafe when developers drop into raw query fragments,

00:00:33.862 --> 00:00:36.517
dynamic clauses, custom sorting, or handwritten filters.

00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:40.311
The assessment question is not whether an ORM is present.

00:00:40.311 --> 00:00:46.380
The question is whether untrusted values can influence query structure rather than being bound as values.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.277
Dynamic features need careful design.

00:00:48.277 --> 00:00:50.174
Sorting, filtering, pagination, search fields,

00:00:50.174 --> 00:00:55.864
and report builders often require parts of a query to change based on user choices.

00:00:55.864 --> 00:01:00.037
These are places where allowlists are more appropriate than free-form input.

00:01:00.037 --> 00:01:00.795
For example,

00:01:00.795 --> 00:01:05.727
a client can be allowed to select from known field names or directions,

00:01:05.727 --> 00:01:09.520
while the application maps those choices to controlled query components.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.693
Input validation helps, but it does not replace safe query construction.

00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:18.625
Validation can reject values that do not match business expectations, reduce accidental errors,

00:01:18.625 --> 00:01:20.142
and improve type safety.

00:01:20.142 --> 00:01:22.418
Parameterization addresses the database interpretation problem.

00:01:22.418 --> 00:01:26.970
Least-privileged database accounts add another layer by limiting what the application can

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.246
do if a query path fails.

00:01:29.246 --> 00:01:32.281
Strong SQL injection prevention is boring by design.

00:01:32.281 --> 00:01:36.074
Use parameterized APIs, constrain dynamic query structure to known choices,

00:01:36.074 --> 00:01:39.489
avoid raw string-built queries, review escape hatches in ORMs,

00:01:39.489 --> 00:01:44.420
and test high-risk paths such as search, filters, sort controls, and administrative reporting.
